{"result": {"response": {"id": "resp_08ef2eb55c5d80f90169bb1810f5088191a47cdfdd373f273e", "created": 1773869072, "model": "gpt-5.2-2025-12-11", "object": "chat.completion", "system_fingerprint": null, "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "message": {"content": "The **rectum (RECTUM)** is a digestive tract organ and pelvic structure that forms the **terminal segment of the large intestine**, located within the **pelvis** alongside organs such as the bladder and reproductive organs. It lies **above the anal canal**, with the **anal canal positioned between the rectum and the anus**, and it functions primarily as a reservoir that **holds stool until it is expelled from the body through the anus**. It is often described as the **last several inches (about 6 inches)** of the large bowel and is listed after the colon as the end portion of the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nClinically, the **rectum** is a key site where **polyps can form** (including in conditions such as **familial adenomatous polyposis [FAP]**, affecting the rectum along with the colon) and where **rectal cancer commonly develops**, frequently arising from polyps or other lesions. Rectal tumors are characterized by **location within the rectum (e.g., upper/mid vs. lower rectum)** and by **depth of invasion through rectal wall layers (including the muscle layer)**, features that influence **staging and surgical planning**. The rectum is also referenced as a potential area involved in **pelvic spread/implants** and may be evaluated for cancer extension into nearby pelvic tissues.\n\nThe **rectum** is routinely assessed with multiple examinations and imaging procedures. It is examined during **digital rectal examination (DRE)**, in which a clinician inserts a gloved finger into the rectum (often assessing the anus and rectum for masses or abnormalities and, in males, palpating the **prostate** for irregularities). It can be examined endoscopically during **proctoscopy** and is traversed during **co